From dede3597b2c001be86cd9b36bc92353044e6db11 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 12:38:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#117): join the tile by identity, and stop the 70%
 bar meaning 70% visible
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A 3D tile scrolled less than roughly 70% into the viewport rendered as raw
SBS — the packed side-by-side buffer drawn as ordinary page content — and
snapped back to correct woven stereo above that. Deterministic, reversible
with scroll position, and present on 0.1.14 as well as the 79-patch
candidate, i.e. pre-existing rather than introduced by 0075-0079.

Two stacked defects, both now fixed.

DEFECT 1 — the identity join has never worked for a real SDK tile.

The GPU id-miss counter climbed roughly per frame (misses=4201 in the
report) even while tiles were weaving CORRECTLY, which means the whole
pipeline was running on the geometric fallback the Phase-1 join was supposed
to demote to a rarity. The reported cause is the duplicate-id line:

  ID-MATCH FALLBACK: reason=duplicate id id=..0001 layer_ids=28/17 kept=28

Patch 0060 diagnosed that split correctly — a kInline3dWeave rect that rides
the element's BACKGROUND paint chunk merges into the enclosing PictureLayer,
while the canvas PIXELS are a ForeignLayer that PendingLayer::RequiresOwnLayer
always keeps separate, so the id cc stamps on the rect can never equal the id
in the SharedQuadState of the quad that draws the canvas. It fixed only half
of it. 0060 stopped ReplacedPainter FORCING a background display item into
existence for an inline-3d-only canvas; it did not stop the item being
recorded when it exists for some OTHER reason, and then
BoxPainter::RecordTrackedElementAndRegionCaptureData hands the chunk the
element's whole TrackedElementSubRects map, kInline3dWeave included.

Which is the normal case, not a corner one. Every tile the inline-3D SDK
creates gets, from Viewer._bindOrbit (displayxr-web js/inline3d-viewer.js):

  el.style.touchAction = 'none';
  el.addEventListener('pointerdown'|'pointermove'|'pointerup'|'pointercancel')
  el.addEventListener('wheel', this._onWheel, { passive: false })

An explicit touch-action makes HasEffectiveAllowedTouchAction() true and the
non-passive wheel listener makes InsideBlockingWheelEventHandler() true, so
ReplacedPainter::ShouldPaintBoxDecorationBackground's earlier conditions paint
the background regardless of 0060's guard — on a canvas with no CSS
decoration at all, as in the model sample (`canvas.tile` is bare: display,
width, aspect-ratio, cursor). Every interactive tile therefore emitted its
tracked rect TWICE, with two layer ids, every frame.

Viz then had to arbitrate, and its arbitration was order-dependent: it kept
the incumbent whenever both copies id-matched or neither did. The rects are
ordered by `std::ranges::sort`, which is NOT stable, and the two copies of one
element differ only in near-identical geometry — so "the incumbent" was
effectively arbitrary, and when the quad-less PictureLayer copy won, the join
died for that element for the whole frame.

Fix: suppress the duplicate AT SOURCE. ReplacedPainter now omits
kInline3dWeave (and only that feature — region capture and everything else is
untouched) from the background chunk exactly when HTMLCanvasPainter will
register it on the canvas's own layer instead, mirroring that painter's
foreign-layer conditions so an unaccelerated / printing / omit-compositing
canvas, where the chunk copy is the ONLY copy, keeps it.

The Viz-side collapse stays, as a should-never-fire guard for an older
renderer or a future painter that re-splits the registration, but it no longer
arbitrates by list order: it ranks the copies (no identity < identity with no
quad < identity whose quad actually covers the tracked rect) and keeps the
best, so even in the degraded case the pixel-owning layer wins.

DEFECT 2 — the geometric fallback was a visibility threshold.

The two halves of that comparison are clipped differently by construction. A
tracked rect reaches the aggregator through EmitSurfaceContent, which
intersects it with the embedding surface's root-target clip, so a tile scrolled
half off the window arrives ALREADY cut down to its visible strip (the report's
1043x412 of a 1043x1043 tile). A candidate quad rect is
`MapEnclosingClippedRect(to_root, quad->rect)` — the layer's FULL unclipped
bounds. best_match normalised the intersection by max(quad_area, target_area),
which for that pair is the quad area, so the score WAS the visible fraction:
412/1043 = 0.395 against a 0.7 bar. No match, no entry in
inline_3d_canvas_resources, so Display::DrawAndSwap suppressed nothing and the
weave had no clean source — the page's own raw SBS quad is what you saw, and
direct_renderer's plane split reported it as tiles_without_k=1.

Fix: score on the COMMON visible space. Both rects are clipped to the root
pass footprint (the browser#94 rect, hoisted above the lambda) before the
ratio, so a 40%-visible tile scores ~1000 against its own quad and the 70% bar
goes back to meaning "is this the same element".

The GPU-side join in canvas_mailbox_for had the same asymmetry — |r| already
window-clipped, |c.rect| the unclipped tile — but there the denominator is the
TARGET area, i.e. a containment test, so it did not reproduce the bug. It is
made symmetric anyway (candidate clipped to window_bounds) and now takes the
BEST candidate rather than the first over the bar, matching the aggregator's
browser#22 tie policy.

Net effect: the id-join succeeds, so the fallback becomes the rare path it was
designed to be, and when it does run it no longer confuses "partly visible"
with "different element". A tile at 10% visibility weaves its visible strip;
the weave already handles window-clipped rects, so nothing new clips it.

